A useful ChatGPT SEO prompt names the task, supplies your source material and states what the output must contain. For example, give a page-rewrite prompt the current copy, intended audience, question to answer and verified evidence; ask it to preserve qualifications and flag missing facts. This produces a draft you can assess, not a guaranteed ranking or citation improvement. My free [SEO prompts tool](/tools/seo-prompts/) contains 20 templates organised by task; this guide explains the inputs and checks to use with them.

## Why Most ChatGPT SEO Prompts Fail

A generic request such as "optimise this page for SEO" can produce advice about keywords, sentences or metadata without identifying the page's actual problem. Give the model the page, user question and evidence, and ask it to explain each proposed change. Check those reasons before using the rewrite.

For a content-planning prompt, [semantic SEO](/services/semantic-seo/) provides a way to organise entities, attributes and user questions. Supply the actual page and query evidence, then ask for missing answers and a proposed structure. Review the output against the sources and existing URLs. This is an editorial workflow, not proof that search engines rank pages by a single entity-coverage formula.

## Eight SEO tasks you can use prompts for

The library covers eight stages of the content and technical SEO workflow. Each prompt is copy-paste ready with bracketed placeholders for your own keyword, URL, or content.

**Keyword and demand research.** Supply real queries from Search Console or a customer-question list. Ask the prompt to group them by intent and task, suggest page types and mark ambiguous rows. If you start from a seed query instead, treat generated related questions as hypotheses to verify, not measured search demand.

**Topical maps and authority.** Supply your existing URLs, their topics and the queries each page serves. Ask for a proposed topic map and gaps, with a reason to update an existing page or create a new one. Check competing page assignments before writing; a ROOT/NODE label alone does not prevent cannibalisation or prove topical authority.

**Content briefs.** Give the model the target question, audience, relevant source extracts and your own verified evidence. Ask for the direct answer, supporting sections, comparison criteria and facts still needing research. If the prompt needs SERP analysis, provide the actual results or use a browsing-capable workflow; the model should not invent what currently ranks.

**Writing and optimisation.** Supply the draft and its verified claims. Ask for clearer answers, meaningful headings and subject names where detached passages would be ambiguous, while retaining exceptions and sources. For titles and descriptions, request accurate alternatives within your chosen editorial limits; character count does not guarantee how Google displays a snippet.

**Schema and technical.** Supply the visible page facts, required schema type and confirmed author or organisation details. Ask for JSON-LD with missing values flagged, then validate the output and check it matches the page before publishing. Generated markup is a draft, not guaranteed valid code or eligibility for a rich result. The [semantic markup guide](/blog/seo-semantic-markup-guide/) explains the implementation.

**Internal linking.** Supply a list of existing URLs and a short description of each page. Ask for contextual link opportunities with a reason, destination and natural anchor text. Open each destination and check that it answers the promised question; reject invented URLs and links that duplicate an existing page's role.

**AEO and GEO.** Give the prompt a target buyer question, the current passage and its supporting sources. Ask whether the passage identifies its subject, answers the question and keeps its scope, date and exceptions with the claim. A clearer passage may be easier to interpret in isolation, but this library has not tested a citation uplift. Google says no special AI markup is required for AI Overviews or AI Mode; see [its AI-feature guidance](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features) and [the LLM optimisation guide](/blog/what-is-llm-optimisation/).

**Local SEO.** Supply the business's real services, address or service area and operating details. Ask for a draft profile description or location-page outline that reflects those facts. Confirm available Business Profile categories in the live product, and remove invented locations, reviews or claims of local experience before using the output.

