## Common Mistakes When Using AI for SEO

**Treating output as finished copy.** AI can help with research, structure and drafts, but the output may still contain errors or miss the user's need. Review its evidence and usefulness before publishing. Every output from this library is a starting point, not a tested ranking intervention.

**Skipping fact verification.** Language models generate plausible-sounding claims that are sometimes wrong. Verify every statistic, date, and factual claim before publishing, especially in Your Money or Your Life topics such as health, finance, or legal content, where an unverified claim carries real risk to the reader and to your site's [E-E-A-T](/blog/what-is-eeat-seo/) signals.

**Running prompts without the page context.** A brief can repeat content you already have or assign the same question to several URLs. Give the model the relevant existing pages, review page ownership and carry verified inputs between tasks. The [topical authority guide](/services/topical-authority/) explains the wider planning approach.

{% pullquote cite="From this guide's common mistakes section" %}
Give the model the relevant existing pages, review page ownership and carry verified inputs between tasks.
{% /pullquote %}

**Skipping output checks when changing models.** The templates can be tried in ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini, but model capability, browsing access and supplied context can change the result. Compare outputs on the same task and verified sources; do not assume any model can see current search results or your analytics automatically.

## Frequently Asked Questions

**Are ChatGPT prompts actually useful for SEO?**

ChatGPT prompts can help draft keyword groups, briefs, rewrites and technical suggestions when you provide the task, source material and required output. Check facts, intent, links and code before using the result. The 20-template library has not been evaluated as a controlled ranking or AI-citation experiment, so a usable draft is the immediate outcome to assess.

**Can I use these prompts with Claude or Gemini instead of ChatGPT?**

Yes. The templates use general instructions rather than a ChatGPT-specific API. Try them in Claude or Gemini with the same source material and checks. Browsing, analytics access and output quality depend on the product, mode and permissions; selecting Gemini does not automatically connect a prompt to your Google data.

**Is AI-generated SEO content ready to publish as-is?**

No. Treat AI-generated SEO content as a draft. Check its facts, sources, usefulness, links and any code before publishing. Accurate data and editing support quality, but do not guarantee search rankings. Take particular care with health, finance and legal claims, where an incorrect answer can harm the reader.

**What order should I run these prompts in?**

Start with verified queries and intent, assign the questions to existing or proposed URLs, then create a brief and draft. Review each output before passing it onward. Finish with fact, link and schema checks. This sequence organises the work; it does not establish that publishing the output will build authority or improve rankings.

